Donald Trump, under fire for record US diesel prices, claims Russia and Ukraine have agreed to stop striking energy targets. Russia said nothing; Ukraine said "we expect specifics from our partners" on any such move.

US President Donald Trump claimed a breakthrough on Monday in diplomacy between Ukraine and Russia , although his unsubstantiated declaration only elicited a very reserved comment from Ukraine and no immediate word from the Kremlin.&#160;

The assertion comes amid indications from both Russia and Ukraine that they would be willing to continue with US-brokered negotiations , which have been largely stagnant throughout Trump's second term.&#160;

Ukraine's Volodymyr Zelenskyy last week told DW that he would be willing to meet with Russian President Vladimir Putin at the G20 summit in Miami in December if rumors of Putin being invited materialized.&#160;

Trump appeared to claim that Ukraine and Russia had agreed to stop hitting each other's energy targets.&#160;

"Ukraine has agreed not to hit Russian Energy targets. Russia has agreed to do, likewise!" Trump or his team wrote.

"The World's Diesel price is mostly caused by the Russia/Ukraine War [sic], not Iran," the Truth Social comment continued, demonstrating — as a quickfire series of agitated&#160;follow-up posts would reinforce — a desire to downplay rising US oil prices,&#160;the role of Trump's war in Iran in pushing them skywards, and economic pressures closer to home.

Ukraine's presidency declined to comment &#160;but President Volodymyr Zelenskyy issued a more guarded statement online.

He did, however, say that he had recommended seeking such&#160;an accord, as winter approaches —&#160;if Russia's cooperation could be ensured after years of it targeting Ukrainian energy infrastructure.&#160;

"Ukraine has proposed that its partners secure an agreement with Russia that would stop the destruction of critical infrastructure," Zelenskyy wrote online. "Food, energy, and other basics that sustain life must be protected, and Russian strikes are directed primarily against them."

But Zelenskyy cautioned that Kyiv "is not convinced that Russia is willing to abide by an agreement," if one could be reached.&#160;

"If our partners are ready to ensure that Russia genuinely refrains from striking our electricity system, other energy facilities, critical infrastructure, and food supply routes, then, of course, we are ready to ensure a corresponding halt to our strikes."&#160;

He said Kyiv would need assurance that this was a lasting deal and not just a bid to secure temporary relief in fuel prices in Russia ahead of elections at the end of the week.&#160;

"We expect specifics from our partners," Zelenskyy concluded, without naming names.&#160;

